 { A concept that is closely linked to schematic thinking is that of paralysis. This is because one side can end up with a paralysed piece (or pieces) due to an unfavorable configuration. Let's show a few examples of this, starting with a simpler position involving only one paralysed piece. Black is winning in a multitude of ways. Find the most forcing and 'cleanest'. } 1... { -KEY- } Kxe4 $1 { Black captures the knight, sacrificing an exchange. The motivation for this is that the rook on a3 is paralysed. } 2. Kxf1 Kf4 $1 ( { Black now picks up the White pawns. Since the pawn endgame after } 2... Kf4 2. Rxa4 { is lost, there is no way for White to put up a fight. } ) 

