 { In spite of the presence of many more pieces on the board, here it is easier to spot paralysis than in the previous example. In spite of being up material, Black is in trouble here since they cannot improve their piece configuration in any way. } 1. { -KEY- } Ne5 $1 { The most human move. If we run through Black's pieces we notice that all of them are stuck: (1) The knight on c7 has no forward jumps as a6 and b5 are covered. Besides, the second it moves White can invade with Rc8. (2) The knight on f8 is stuck since ...Nd7 is the only square, but that would allow Rxc7. (3) The queen has to shuffle between the d6, d8 and e7 squares in order to keep the knight defended. (4) The king is also stuck, due to White's grip on the g6 & f7 squares. } Ne8 { Black loses his patience and moves the knight. } 2. Rc8 Nd6 3. Ra8 { Now the rook is even more active. The threat is to bring either knight to g6 next. } g5 { Black is forced to give up a pawn to stop the mating threat. } 4. hxg6+ Kg7 5. Nh5+ Kg8 6. g7 $1 { Carlsen converts in the simplest way, forcing an endgame where he will be up a lot of material. } Ne8 7. gxf8=R+ Qxf8 8. g4 ( 8. Rxe8 { would win the queen immediately. Perhaps Carlsen missed it, or perhaps he was savoring the fact that his opponent still remained paralysed in spite of having given up material trying to break free. } ) 
fxg4 9. Nxg4 Kf7 10. Rxa7+ { Black resigned. Let's take a look at a very challenging example next to show how difficult things can sometimes get! } 
