 { Our previous example was quite straightforward. Most of the time things will not be so easy. In this position Magnus Carlsen had the White pieces against GM Hakobyan and failed to find the only winning move. } 1. { -KEY- } Bd4 $3 { A very difficult move to find, since it required spotting that Black had a way of breaking the deadly grip of White's piece arrangement by playing ...b4 and then ...Re8. Of course, in Carlsen's case the reason for missing it is that the game was played with short time controls. } ( 1. g5 $2 { was played in the game, but after } fxg5 2. Bd4 Rf8 $17 { Suddenly White is the one with the worse position, as the rooks are starting to come into play. } ) 
 ( 1. h4 { A quiet waiting move also fails, since Black has a key idea in the form of } b4 $1 { when the point is that after } 2. Bxb4 ( 2. cxb4 { is not worrying since in that case White loses his connected pawns. } ) 
Re8 $1 { The pawn sacrifice on b4 has distracted the bishop, which can no longer cover the e5 square. } ) 
b4 { Now this fails. } ( 1... Rc8 { is another possible try. White wins after } 2. Ra1 $1 Rc6 3. Bc5 b4 4. cxb4 $1 { Without a Black rook able to slide to the e-file, capturing with the pawn is now winning. } Rc8 5. Re1 $1 Re8 { Black gets to the file short one tempo. } 6. Rxe8 Kxe8 7. Kc6 $18 { Black will not be able to stop both passed pawns. } ) 
 ( 1... Re8 { Black can also sit and wait. Here White wins quickly due to zugzwang. } 2. Ra1 $1 { Black has no moves. If: } ( 2... b4 { then } 3. c4 { with deadly connected pawns. } ) 
 ( 2... Re2 { then } 3. Ra8 $1 { invading along the last rank } ) 
 ( 2... Rbb8 { then } 3. Ra7 ) 
Rd8 { Black waits along the back rank, but now after } 3. Re1 { the threat is 4.Re7+ with the decisive invasion. The only way to contest this is with } Re8 { but now White can exchange rooks and invade with his king. } 4. Rxe8 Kxe8 5. Kc6 $1 Rd7 { Black is stuck guarding the d-pawn. } 6. h4 $1 { Anticipating that Black's only source of counterplay is the ...g6 pawn break. } g6 7. fxg6 hxg6 8. h5 $1 { This is the idea that White had in mind when playing h4 a couple of moves earlier. } gxh5 9. gxh5 Rh7 10. Bxf6 Rf7 11. Be5 Rh7 12. Bd4 $1 { White waits to force the rook to move away from the h-file. } ( 11... b4 11. h6 $4 Rxh6 $11 ) 
Rd7 13. h6 Rh7 14. Bg7 $1 { Just when Black thought the paralysis could not get worse...! } Kd8 15. d7 $18 { White will promote his pawn soon. } ) 
2. c4 $1 b3 3. Bb2 $1 { This is the clever point of 1.Bd4. Now White keeps his connected passed pawns while keeping Black's b-pawn under control. } Rc8 4. c5 Rc6 5. Ra1 Rc8 6. Re1 ( { In this case Black cannot play } 6. Re1 Re8 { offering a rook trade due to } 7. c6 { . } ) 
Rb5 { Black has to go after the c5 pawn. } 7. Re7+ Kd8 8. Rxg7 Rcxc5+ 9. Ke6 { This time it's a different configuration, but one that is just as deadly. White wants 10.Rg8#. } Kc8 10. d7+ { Many moves win, but this is the most direct path. } Kc7 11. Rg8 Re5+ { Black has to give back the exchange to stop the pawn, but this allows White to transition to a winning rook and pawn endgame. } 12. Bxe5+ Rxe5+ 13. Kxf6 Rd5 14. Rc8+ Kxd7 15. Rb8 { White has given up the d-pawn, but now has the rook ideally placed behind the dangerous b-pawn. } Rd3 16. Kg7 $18 { Black cannot stop the f-pawn from marching forward. This example, and the associated lines, seems like a position befitting a Dvoretsky book. Such cases are rare, most of the time detecting paralysis will be easier. Let's take a look at a more common level of difficulty in the next example. } 
