 { The following position was reached in a game where the White pieces were being played by a man from British India, by the name of Mir Sultan Khan. If you have not heard this name before, you may find his story a fascinating one. He was a servant hailing from Punjab, who learned modern chess only when he was brought to Europe by his nobleman master. With little English and little knowledge of opening theory, he nevertheless quickly established himself as one of the world's strongest players. In his brief five-year career, he amassed a list of impressive achievements, with victories over several of the world's best players. One of his most impressive victories was at the Hastings 1930/31 tournament where he defeated the great Jose Raul Capablanca. In that game, the following position was reached by move 47. } 47. { -KEY- } Bf4 { Black is paralysed. There is no way to move the bishop or the knight. The king is also limited in squares while the most free piece is the queen. However, she cannot pose any problems as there are no weaknesses in the White position for her to target. Given this, all that is left for White is to identify the right schema in order to make the decisive breakthrough. Let's see how Mir Sultan Khan goes about this. } Qh3 48. Kb2 Qg2 49. Kb1 Qh3 ( 49... Qxf2 $2 { is not possible due to } 50. Bh5 $1 { when after } Qh4 51. Rh2 { the queen is lost. } ) 
50. Ka1 Qg2 51. Kb2 { Perhaps White was shuffling the king while trying to decide how to make a breakthrough. } Qh3 52. Rg1 $1 { Threatening to lock in the queen. } Bc8 { Black has to stop 53.Bg4, but now this opens up the c6 square. } 53. Rc6 $1 Qh4 54. Rgc1 Bg4 55. Bf1 Qh5 ( 55... Qxf2 56. R6c2 { wins material, since } Qh4 { runs into } 57. Rh2 { while } Qg1 { can be met by } 57. Rg2 { . } ) 
56. Re1 ( 56. Rxb6 { I suspect the reason why White did not capture the pawn immediately was the fear that Black would make a perpetual after } Be2 $1 57. Bxe2 $4 Qxe2 58. Rc2 Qe1 $1 { when suddenly the game is drawn as White cannot escape checks on b4 and e1. } ) 
Qh1 57. Rec1 Qh5 58. Kc3 ( { A clever plan. White prepares to meet } 58. Kc3 Be2 { with } 59. Kd2 { , when the king will sit safe from perpetual checks. } ) 
Qh4 59. Bg3 Qxg5 60. Kd2 { White has given up the g5 pawn in order to make sure no perpetual check is possible. } Qh5 61. Rxb6 Ke7 62. Rb7+ Ke6 63. b6 Nf6 { The Black knight is able to move at last, but by now White has made too much progress for it to matter. } 64. Bb5 Qh3 65. Rb8 { Black resigned, since the b-pawn will promote soon. In this game, we saw how White's configuration was very harmonious, leaving very few targets for the Black queen. At the same time, the remaining pieces were immobilised. From here, the only thing left to do was to find the right piece arrangement to make a decisive breakthrough on the queenside. Next, we'll discuss the concept of 'Fortresses', which is closely related to Paralysis. Let's take a look at two examples to close out the section. } 
 1-0

