 { I reached this position in the game Thorhallsson, T - Astaneh Lopez,A (2014) . Black is facing some pressure due to the more active placement of White's pieces. In the game I failed to spot a potential fortress and quickly lost. } 33... { -KEY- } bxc3 $1 { The start of a queen sacrifice, with the goal of establishing a fortress. } ( { In the game I quickly went wrong, playing } 33... Kh8 $2 { when after } 34. dxe5 { I immediately blundered with } Nxe5 $4 { Here, White played } 35. Ng3 $1 { when Black cannot avoid losing the pinned knight: } f6 36. f4 $18 ) 
34. bxc3 exd4 $1 35. Nf6+ { White has nothing better than to win the queen. } ( 35. cxd4 { was another try, but it leads to the same outcome. } Nxd4 $1 { Insisting on the fortress! } 36. Nf6 gxf6 37. Rxe6 Rxe6 38. Rd5 Rd6 $1 39. Rxd6 cxd6 40. Qd5 Ne6 41. Qxd6 f5 $11 { In spite of White's material advantage and my fractured pawns, there is no way to break down Black's defensive configuration. For example: } 42. Qe5 Ng7 43. g3 h5 $11 { The knight defends both pawns, while the king covers f7. Since the Black rook controls the third rank, there is also no way for the White king to penetrate into the position to add piece pressure to the weak pawns. } ) 
Rxf6 36. Rxf6 dxe3 $1 37. Rxe6 Rxe6 38. fxe3 Ne5 { The knight is ideally placed on the weak square on e5. } 39. Qd1 c6 $11 { The fortress has been set up. The knight defends the f7 and c6 pawns, while the rook defends the knight and prevents the White king from progressing beyond the third rank. White can push his g- and h-pawns up the board, but this will not bother Black since his 3 vs 2 pawn majority can easily hold them at bay. The position is drawn. In this section we covered two important concepts related to schemas: paralysis, and fortresses. In the remaining sections of the chapter, we'll shift the focus away from schematic thinking and into two other important topics: the role of pawns in the endgame, and the role of the king. See you in the next section! } 
